    "available_at": "2026-11-16T20:00:21.980399+00:00",
    "body": "Most senior decision-makers in a recent energy-industry survey think today’s electricity demand forecasts are too low. The poll, commissioned for ADIPEC, which bills itself as “the world’s largest energy event,” found that 81% of 1,528 respondents expect artificial intelligence (AI)-driven demand to exceed current forecasts, and 32% expect it to be much higher. The harder question is whether the wires, transformers, and permits will be ready if demand does.\nA separate study, The Energy Readiness Equation from engineering and consulting firm Jacobs, puts numbers on that gap. Among 600 energy-sector decision-makers in the U.S., U.K., Germany, Singapore, and Australia, 71% said sufficient capital is available in their geography. Only 29% said there is both enough capital and enough investable projects. In the U.S., one in five said their organization consistently delivers major infrastructure on time and on budget, compared with 30% globally, and 80% ranked permitting and consenting among their three biggest barriers. ADIPEC’s respondents pointed to a related choke point: Delays in building and connecting grids were the second-biggest barrier to meeting demand after geopolitics, with supply chain and equipment constraints close behind.\nThat raises a question the industry has been slow to ask: How much more can the infrastructure already in the ground carry? State regulators are beginning to write that question into formal proceedings.\nThe NC Clean Energy Technology Center’s 50 States of Grid Modernization report for the third quarter of 2026 counted 411 grid modernization actions across 43 states, the District of Columbia, and Puerto Rico. The tally includes dockets and proposals as well as enacted policy, so it measures activity rather than results. The first trend the authors identify is a push for metrics and planning rules on “grid utilization,” which they define as how efficiently a utility uses its existing infrastructure to serve load.\nVirginia lawmakers were the first in the country to order grid utilization metrics earlier this year, according to the report, and others are following. California enacted a bill that makes utility data on utilization opportunities public and requires utilities to assess whether such solutions can be cost-effective alternatives within distribution planning. Minnesota regulators ordered Xcel Energy to build utilization metrics into its next Integrated Distribution Plan. Colorado regulators opened a rulemaking under which distribution system plans must include a scenario that uses load flexibility to increase capacity utilization.\nFlexible interconnection is the practical counterpart. Xcel Energy has filed a proposal in Colorado that would offer both static and dynamic flexible interconnection. Customers could enroll temporarily until the company completes system upgrades, or permanently as their long-term connection method. On the transmission side, New Jersey enacted a law requiring utilities to consider advanced transmission technologies in transmission project applications, with third-party cost-effectiveness testing to justify any decision to exclude them. Ohio regulators approved a cost-effectiveness methodology that counts reduced system losses and avoided upgrades, and Indiana regulators published a study covering nine categories of the technologies.\n“Policymakers are increasingly interested in advanced transmission technologies, which allow utilities to improve usage of the grid without having to replace the existing infrastructure,” Brian Lips, a senior project manager at the center, said in a release accompanying the report.\nThe Jacobs report offers an example of the approach in practice. Guy Nicholson, head of zero carbon grid solutions at Statkraft, argues that connection constraints can reflect conservative assumptions, historic network data, or standardized processes rather than an absolute physical limit. By examining the network data, equipment capability, and operating conditions behind a connection offer, and working through those constraints with the system operator, Statkraft shortened the initial connection timeline for a battery project from seven years to two, according to the report. Part of the solution was accepting that the facility could be curtailed during the limited periods when the constraint genuinely arose.\n“Once you understand what’s happening on the network, you can start to solve the problem from an engineering point of view: where does it overload, how do we protect it, and what can we do about it?” Nicholson said in the report.\nThat cuts both ways. A flexible connection trades some firmness for time, and the trade is easier to make when the system has room to spare. Jacobs notes that rapid load growth is eroding that room. “The resilience preparedness gap is real and gets much bigger as the system gets tighter,” Will Hazelip, U.S. president of National Grid, said in the Jacobs report.\nThe survey data suggest the gap is already visible. About 95% of Jacobs’ respondents said they are somewhat or very confident their critical assets would keep operating through a major disruption tomorrow, yet only 36% described their approach to resilience as proactive and embedded. The report also cautions that optimizing exclusively for speed can push economics, efficiency, resilience, and emissions down the decision hierarchy.\nNone of this replaces new generation, new lines, and new equipment, and none of those will arrive as fast as buyers want. But the questions commissions are starting to ask can be acted on now: How hard is the existing grid working, and where is capacity sitting idle?\nJacobs frames the shift as a move from asking “When can this project receive an unconstrained connection?” to “What can be connected safely now, and under what conditions?” The second question is the more useful one. The test will be whether utilities, regulators, and large loads accept the conditions that come with the answer, and whether anyone keeps an honest count of what the curtailed hours cost.\n—Aaron Larson is POWER’s executive editor.\nTagged in:\nADIPEC\nColorado\ncurtailment\nFlexible interconnection\ngrid modernization\nGrid utilization\nJacobs\nstatkraft\nVirginia\nXcel Energy\nSept. 28—30, 2026\nWashington, D.C.\nRegister Now\nExplore this topic and more — live at Experience POWER. The power industry's most urgent decisions are being made right now. Be in the room where they happen.\nView Conference Program",
